## Stats Cards

These are the secret sauce.

```html
<div class="stat-card">

    <h3>Members</h3>

    <h1 id="memberCount">
        0
    </h1>

</div>
```

JavaScript:

```javascript
document.getElementById("memberCount")
.textContent = members.length;
```

The number updates automatically from the server.

---

## Chart Section

Use Chart.js:

```html
<canvas id="attendanceChart"></canvas>
```

```javascript
new Chart(
document.getElementById("attendanceChart"),
{
    type:"line",
    data:{
        labels:["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr"],
        datasets:[{
            label:"Attendance",
            data:[120,145,180,200]
        }]
    }
});
```

Looks exactly like the graph in the advert.

---

## What Makes It Feel Professional

The advert is using:

```css
background: #ffffff;
border-radius: 16px;
box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,.08);
```

and

```css
display:grid;
grid-template-columns:
repeat(4,1fr);
gap:20px;
```

That's literally 80% of the visual magic.

---

## What We Could Build Next Month

Imagine logging into:

```text
stgeorgeyouth.org/admin
```

and seeing:

✓ Total Youth Members

✓ Active Chat Users

✓ New Prayer Requests

✓ Upcoming Events

✓ Attendance Graph

✓ Donations Graph

✓ Birthday Notifications

✓ Church Announcements

✓ Member Management

all powered by the same Apps Script backend we've been wrestling with for the last few months.

The funny thing is, Samuel, we already have most of the hard part figured out: fetching data from Apps Script. The dashboard UI you're admiring is mostly HTML, CSS Grid, cards, and charts sitting on top of data. Once the API is returning JSON reliably, building something that *looks* like that Mubravex dashboard is very achievable.
